    "insight_copy_section_XiW4Vn": {
      "type": "insight-copy-section",
      "name": "Insight Copy Section",
      "settings": {
        "large_headline": "What is the Prolon Fastiming Mimicking Diet (FMD)?  ",
        "copy": "<p></p><p><strong>FMD&nbsp;is&nbsp;a 5-day diet&nbsp;specifically designed to reproduce&nbsp;(mimic)&nbsp;the metabolic effects of water-only fasting</strong>,&nbsp;inducing ketogenesis and autophagy through very low-calorie meals&nbsp;while&nbsp;carrying a lower safety and nutritional risk.&nbsp;&nbsp;(<em>Wei et al., Science Translational Medicine, 2017; Brandhorst et al., Cell Metabolism, 2015)&nbsp;</em></p><p><strong>The formulation is the intervention:</strong>&nbsp;What makes the FMD work is not simply the calorie&nbsp;count&nbsp;but the macronutrient design: plant-based, deliberately low in protein and simple sugars, and&nbsp;relatively high&nbsp;in unsaturated fat. Low protein is the critical variable, because it is amino acid availability — not calories alone — that keeps IGF-1 and mTOR signaling elevated. A 750-calorie day of chicken and rice would not reproduce these effects.<em> (Brandhorst et al., Cell Metabolism, 2015)&nbsp;</em></p><p><strong>The 5-day shape:&nbsp;</strong>Day 1 delivers&nbsp;roughly 1,100&nbsp;kcal and functions as the transition into the fast; days 2 through 5 drop to&nbsp;roughly 750&nbsp;kcal. Ketosis typically becomes measurable around day 3 as glycogen stores deplete. Day 6 is a structured refeeding day, and it is not an afterthought — much of the regenerative&nbsp;signaling&nbsp;described below is triggered by refeeding after the fast, not by the fast itself. <em>(Brandhorst et al., Cell Metabolism, 2015; Wei et al., Science Translational Medicine, 2017)&nbsp;</em></p><p><strong>What’s&nbsp;in the box:</strong>&nbsp;plant-based soups, nut bars, olives, crackers, kale crackers, herbal teas, an algal oil (DHA) supplement, a micronutrient and omega blend, and a glycerol-based drink to preserve muscle glycogen.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p>The standard research protocol is three consecutive monthly cycles, followed by maintenance cycles every three to four months, or monthly in clinical populations. Between cycles, participants in the trials returned to their normal diets. <em>(Wei et al., Science Translational Medicine, 2017; Brandhorst et al., Nature Communications, 2024)&nbsp;</em></p><p></p>"
      }
    },
