    "insight_copy_section_mw8cE4": {
      "type": "insight-copy-section",
      "name": "Insight Copy Section",
      "settings": {
        "large_headline": "Why we like it: Health Benefits ",
        "copy": "<p><strong>Rigorously tested across many different disease pathologies</strong>,&nbsp;especially cardiovascular disease, diabetes, cognitive impairment including Alzheimer’s dementia, and autoimmune diseases including multiple sclerosis, Crohn’s&nbsp;disease&nbsp;and various cancers. As of&nbsp;2025&nbsp;the human&nbsp;literature includes at least eleven randomized controlled trials, pooled in a systematic review and meta-analysis. (Diabetology & Metabolic Syndrome, 2025)&nbsp;</p><p><strong>Cardiovascular Risk:&nbsp;</strong>In a 100-participant crossover&nbsp;trial, the&nbsp;FMD simultaneously improved multiple modifiable risk factors associated with cardiovascular disease, including blood pressure, adiposity (trunk/visceral fat), fasting glucose and insulin resistance, atherogenic lipids (triglycerides, total and LDL cholesterol), IGF-1, and CRP.  (Wei et al., Science Translational Medicine, 2017)&nbsp;</p><p><strong>Glycemic Control:</strong>&nbsp;In a 12-month randomized controlled trial, monthly FMD cycles integrated into routine primary care for 100 patients with type 2 diabetes reduced the need for glucose-lowering medication while simultaneously improving HbA1c and body weight. Glycemic management&nbsp;improved in&nbsp;53% of FMD participants versus 8% of controls. (Diabetologia, 2024)&nbsp;</p><p><strong>Body Composition:&nbsp;</strong>The fasting mimicking diet is also great for&nbsp;optimizing&nbsp;body composition, reducing&nbsp;adiposityand preserving muscle mass.&nbsp;&nbsp;(Wei et al., Science Translational Medicine, 2017; Sadeghian et al., Obesity Surgery, 2021;&nbsp;npj&nbsp;Metabolic Health and Disease, 2023)&nbsp;</p><p><strong>Skin Health:</strong>&nbsp;In&nbsp;a first ever clinical trial on skin health,&nbsp;3 monthly cycles of the&nbsp;FMD demonstrated improvements in skin hydration&nbsp;and&nbsp;preservation of skin texture.  (Maloh&nbsp;et al., Journal of Clinical Medicine, 2023)&nbsp;</p><p><strong>Hunger & Satiety Regulation:</strong>&nbsp;&nbsp;FMD&nbsp;lowers leptin (reflecting fat loss and improved sensitivity) and, per pooled fasting-regimen data, may lower rather than raise ghrelin, with ketosis providing a mechanistic basis for appetite suppression.&nbsp;&nbsp;(Sadeghian et al., Obesity Surgery, 2021; Tavakoli et al., Journal of Health, Population and Nutrition, 2025)&nbsp;</p><p><strong>Gut Microbiome Health:</strong>&nbsp;While research on this area is&nbsp;emerging&nbsp;(mostly preclinical), studies show that&nbsp;FMD cycles remodel gut bacterial composition toward an anti-inflammatory, barrier-protective profile, most reproducibly by expanding&nbsp;Lactobacillaceae&nbsp;and short-chain-fatty-acid (SCFA)–producing commensals.&nbsp;&nbsp;(Rangan et al., Cell Reports, 2019)&nbsp;</p><p><strong>Biological age.&nbsp;</strong>While biological-age clocks are surrogate markers&nbsp;rather than a firm indicator or mortality, pooled analysis of two trials (n=100 and n=44; Brandhorst et al., Nature Communications, 2024) found a median 2.5-year reduction in biological age after three cycles, independent of weight loss. Hepatic fat fraction fell by&nbsp;roughly half&nbsp;in participants with fatty liver (14.3% → 7.9%), insulin resistance improved, and the lymphoid&nbsp;to-myeloid ratio rose — a marker of immune profile shifting toward a younger phenotype&nbsp;</p><p><strong>Autoimmune and inflammatory&nbsp;disease.&nbsp;</strong>In a recent randomized controlled trial in 97 adults with mild-to-moderate Crohn’s disease (Kulkarni&nbsp;et. Al.,&nbsp;Nature Medicine, 2026),&nbsp;three monthly FMD cycles&nbsp;in combination with medical interventions significantly&nbsp;improved the disease state (CDAI)&nbsp;compared to a control group that&nbsp;did not make changes to their dietary&nbsp;pattern.&nbsp;These benefits were&nbsp;achieved without increased therapy escalation.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p><strong>Overall</strong>:&nbsp;The FMD is one of few nutrition interventions that addresses foundational metabolic health, body composition and aesthetic aging within a single protocol — and one of very few dietary interventions of any kind with randomized evidence spanning healthy adults, type 2 diabetes, autoimmune disease and oncology.&nbsp;</p>"
      }
    },
