    "insight_copy_section_G6tzyd": {
      "type": "insight-copy-section",
      "name": "Insight Copy Section",
      "settings": {
        "large_headline": "How it works: Overview of key concepts ",
        "copy": "<p><strong>Ketogenesis:</strong>&nbsp;Around day 2 to 3, depleted glycogen forces a shift from glucose to fatty acid oxidation, and the liver begins producing ketone bodies. Ketones are not only an alternative fuel: beta-hydroxybutyrate is a signaling molecule and an endogenous histone deacetylase inhibitor, influencing gene expression related to oxidative stress resistance. This is the biochemical evidence that the FMD reproduces a genuine fasting state rather than merely restricting calories. <em>(Shimazu et al., Science, 2013)&nbsp;</em></p><p><strong>Modulating&nbsp;IGF-1:&nbsp;</strong>Insulin-like growth factor-1 (IGF-1), often described as the body’s primary growth messenger, is a peptide hormone produced&nbsp;mainly by&nbsp;the liver and responsible for upregulating growth and proliferative pathways. It is particularly important during growth and development, playing a role in muscle growth, bone building, cellular&nbsp;regeneration&nbsp;and wound healing. While IGF-1 continues to support structural and tissue integrity as we age, staying within a normal range (not too low, not too high) becomes increasingly important, as elevated IGF-1 has been consistently associated with several cancers as well as cardiometabolic disease (type 2 diabetes, coronary artery disease), essentially accelerating&nbsp;the aging process.&nbsp;IGF-1 and the pro-aging cellular pathways downstream of it (RAS, PI3K, AKT and MAPK) all depend on the body’s supply of glucose and amino acids — the same supply that provides cancer cells with a consistent source of energy. The FMD&nbsp;emerged&nbsp;from lab research showing that by lowering circulating glucose, IGF-1 and insulin, prolonged fasting modulates oncogenic pathways, disrupts the&nbsp;tumour&nbsp;microenvironment, and activates protective effects including cellular turnover and regeneration of healthy cells.&nbsp;IGF-1 suppression is the most consistently replicated biochemical effect of the FMD: circulating IGF-1, glucose and insulin fall after roughly&nbsp;48 hours&nbsp;without food <em>(Lee et al., Science Translational Medicine, 2012; Brandhorst et al., Cell Metabolism, 2015; Diabetology & Metabolic Syndrome, 2025)&nbsp;</em></p><p><strong>Autophagy:&nbsp;</strong>Autophagy is the cell’s self-digestion and recycling program&nbsp;(controlled breakdown of damaged proteins, dysfunctional&nbsp;mitochondria&nbsp;and cellular debris, which are then reused as raw material).&nbsp;mTOR, a nutrient-sensing kinase&nbsp;which down-regulates this process, becomes active during nutrition abundance.&nbsp;When amino acids,&nbsp;glucose&nbsp;and insulin decrease in the body, so does mTOR activity, allowing for autophagy&nbsp;to take place.&nbsp;Autophagy declines with age, and its impairment is implicated in neurodegenerative,&nbsp;metabolicand oncological disease. <em>(GeroScience, 2025)&nbsp;</em></p><p><strong>Differential Stress Resistance:&nbsp;</strong>DSR is the observation that not all cells respond to nutrient scarcity the same way. When food is withdrawn, a healthy cell reads the signal correctly,&nbsp;downregulates growth pathways, enters a protected low-metabolism state, and diverts energy&nbsp;from proliferation into repair, antioxidant&nbsp;defense&nbsp;and DNA maintenance. Cells with dysregulated growth signaling cannot make that switch. The mutations driving them (in RAS, PI3K/AKT and related pathways) hold the growth machinery in the on position regardless of whether fuel is&nbsp;actually available, so they keep dividing into a stress they have no protection against.&nbsp;Everyone accumulates damaged,&nbsp;senescent&nbsp;and dysfunctional cells with age, and DSR describes a selective pressure that healthy cells pass and compromised ones fail. This is the mechanistic case for periodic fasting as a form of cellular quality control rather than simple calorie reduction:&nbsp;it imposes a test that only metabolically flexible cells can pass.<em> (Raffaghello et al., PNAS, 2008; Lee et al., Science Translational Medicine, 2012)&nbsp;</em></p><p><strong>Differential Stress Sensitization</strong>:&nbsp;Where DSR describes healthy cells becoming more resistant, DSS describes compromised cells becoming more vulnerable&nbsp;due to lacking&nbsp;metabolic flexibility. A healthy cell can switch from glucose to fatty acids and ketones when glucose runs short. Cells with dysregulated growth signaling are locked into glucose dependence and cannot. Remove the glucose and growth signals they rely on, and they are left exposed with no adaptive response available,&nbsp;prompting to&nbsp;the progressively weaken and ‘self-destruct’.&nbsp;The same five days that move healthy cells into a protected maintenance state move dysfunctional ones toward failure. This&nbsp;asymmetryis what separates periodic fasting from ordinary&nbsp;caloric restriction. Paired with autophagy, which clears damaged cellular components, and the regeneration that follows refeeding, DSS completes a clear-out-and-rebuild cycle: apply&nbsp;a stress&nbsp;that disadvantages what is broken, then supply the conditions to replace it. <em>(Lee et al., Science Translational Medicine, 2012)&nbsp;</em></p><p>The direct evidence&nbsp;for DSS and DSR&nbsp;was&nbsp;established&nbsp;through&nbsp;cancer research, where&nbsp;cytotoxic drugs like chemotherapy&nbsp;make the sensitization visible in a way that fasting alone does not.&nbsp;The fasting mimicking diet continues to be explored as a supportive (adjunct) therapy to cancer therapy,&nbsp;retarding&nbsp;tumor growth and sensitizinga range of cancer cell types to chemotherapy.&nbsp;&nbsp;<em>(Lee et al., Science Translational Medicine, 2012; de Groot et al., Nature Communications, 2020)</em>&nbsp;</p><p><strong>Stem cell activation and the refeeding phase.&nbsp;</strong>While the fast drives catabolic breakdown and clearance over a 5-day period,&nbsp;the&nbsp;refeeding&nbsp;period&nbsp;drives regeneration. In the preclinical&nbsp;research, FMD cycles followed by refeeding produced a five-fold increase in mesenchymal stem/progenitor cells and a 45-fold increase in actively dividing ones, alongside hippocampal neurogenesis and a ten-fold induction of hepatic proliferation markers.&nbsp;Related biomarkers&nbsp;observed&nbsp;in&nbsp;human data on immune profiling&nbsp;(the shift in lymphoid-to-myeloid ratio)&nbsp;shows&nbsp;consistencywith this regenerative model.&nbsp;This is why in our practice, we&nbsp;emphasize the importance of a transition diet, during which&nbsp;minimally processed, nutrient-dense and antioxidant rich foods are prioritized.&nbsp;&nbsp;<em>(Cheng et al., Cell Stem Cell, 2014; Brandhorst et al., Cell Metabolism, 2015)&nbsp;</em></p>"
      }
    },
    "insight_highlight_1_dNA9VY": {
      "type": "insight-highlight-1",
      "name": "Insight Highlight 1",
      "settings": {
        "heading": "",
        "content": "<p>The fasting mimicking diet is one of the few nutrition interventions that, through a repeatable five-day commitment, has&nbsp;accumulated randomized evidence across genuinely distinct clinical domains: glycemic control, cardiovascular risk, autoimmune&nbsp;inflammation&nbsp;and oncology adjunct therapy. Very few dietary protocols of any kind have been tested that broadly.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>"
      }
    },
    "insight_copy_section_GHXkmT": {
      "type": "insight-copy-section",
      "name": "Insight Copy Section",
      "settings": {
        "large_headline": "Conclusion",
        "copy": "<p>The fasting mimicking diet is one of the few nutrition interventions that, through a repeatable five-day commitment, has&nbsp;accumulated randomized evidence across genuinely distinct clinical domains: glycemic control, cardiovascular risk, autoimmune&nbsp;inflammation&nbsp;and oncology adjunct therapy. Very few dietary protocols of any kind have been tested that broadly.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p>The evidence is also more specific than the marketing around it.&nbsp;Cumulative&nbsp;data is most robust on biomarkers&nbsp;(HbA1c, IGF-1, blood pressure)&nbsp;and least consistent on the outcomes people most often&nbsp;buy it&nbsp;for: weight, waist&nbsp;circumference&nbsp;and lipids. This distinction is worth reading as a signal&nbsp;rather than a shortfall.<strong> The FMD behaves like a metabolic and cellular intervention that happens to produce weight loss, not a weight-loss product.&nbsp;&nbsp;</strong></p><p>It also works hardest where there is most to correct.&nbsp;The largest effects appear in people who start furthest from a healthy baseline&nbsp;(patients with type 2 diabetes reducing medication while improving control, Crohn's patients reaching remission at&nbsp;nearly twice&nbsp;the control rate, participants with fatty liver halving hepatic fat fraction). In already-healthy&nbsp;people&nbsp;the effects are real but more modest, which makes the FMD better understood as a restorative intervention than an optimizing one.&nbsp;</p><p>While few clinical trials on the FMD have&nbsp;follow&nbsp;participants beyond a year, which for now means we should continue repeating FMD cycles periodically as a maintenance approach.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p>For now, the FMD is the most rigorously studied periodic fasting protocol available, and one of the few dietary interventions whose mechanism is characterized well enough to predict who will benefit and why.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p>The FMD&nbsp;does not replace the&nbsp;important daily habits (nutrition, sleep, movement)&nbsp;that determine long-term health. It is a time-bound, well-evidenced addition to them, and for the right person, an unusually efficient place to begin.&nbsp;</p>"
      }
    },
